course: geography
lesson: 1
title: Maps and scale
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A map is the world shrunk by the same amount everywhere, so a ruler on paper can still measure real distances.
explain: The scale tells you how much it was shrunk: 1:50,000 means 1 cm on the paper is 50,000 cm, or 500 metres, on the ground.
explain: Think of the EU5 map: zoomed right out you see the whole world with little detail, like a small-scale map; zoomed in on one province you see every river bend, like a large-scale map.
explain: But the game prints no scale at all, so you judge distance by how many locations an army marches through, not by kilometres, and a real map is far more exact.
explain: A key explains the symbols, contour lines show how high and steep the land is, and grid references let two people point to exactly the same square.
explain: Only a globe gets shapes, sizes, distances and directions all right at once; every flat map, the game's included, bends something.
fact: A map scale of 1:50,000 means that 1 cm on the map stands for 50,000 cm, which is 500 metres, on the real ground.
fact: A large-scale map, such as 1:25,000, shows a small area in great detail, while a small-scale map, such as 1:10,000,000, shows a huge area with little detail.
fact: Great Britain's national mapping agency, Ordnance Survey, began in 1791 and makes detailed maps at scales of 1:25,000 and 1:50,000.
fact: Contour lines join points of equal height above sea level, and lines drawn close together mean the ground is steep.
fact: On British maps a four-figure grid reference gives the eastings (the number along the bottom) first and the northings (the number up the side) second.
fact: Maps use a key, also called a legend, that explains the symbols, colours and lines, such as blue for water and a cross for a church.
fact: A political map shows countries, borders and cities, while a physical map shows natural features such as mountains, rivers and heights.
fact: Only a globe can show the shapes, areas, distances and directions of all places correctly at the same time.
fact: The Earth is about 40,000 km around the equator, and the Greek scholar Eratosthenes estimated this size quite well around 240 BC.
fact: From the late 1200s, sailors in the Mediterranean used portolan charts, sea maps with coastlines and criss-crossing compass lines.
fact: A satnav or phone finds its position by timing radio signals from at least four satellites in orbit.
fact: EU5 shows hills and mountains as terrain pictures, a rough version of what contour lines show exactly, and its map modes are like maps with different keys laid over the same land.
fact: Educated people in the Middle Ages, the time when EU5 begins, already knew the Earth was round; the idea that they thought it flat is a myth.
exercise: On a 1:50,000 map, what does 1 cm stand for on the ground? | 500 metres | 50 metres | 5 kilometres | 50 kilometres
exercise: On a 1:25,000 map, how far on the ground is 4 cm on the paper? | 1 kilometre | 100 metres | 4 kilometres | 25 kilometres
exercise: Which map shows a small area in the most detail? | A 1:25,000 map | A 1:10,000,000 map | A 1:1,000,000 map | A 1:250,000 map
exercise: Which would you take on a hike in the Lake District? | A large-scale map such as 1:25,000 | A small-scale map of all of Europe | A world map from an atlas | A map with no scale at all
exercise: When did Britain's Ordnance Survey begin? | In 1791 | In 1066 | In 1914 | In 1966
exercise: What do contour lines join? | Points of the same height above sea level | Places with the same temperature on a given day | Towns linked by the same road | Points of the same distance from the sea
exercise: If contour lines are drawn very close together, what is the ground like? | Steep | Flat | Wet and marshy | Covered in forest
exercise: In a four-figure grid reference, which number comes first? | The easting, along the bottom | The northing, up the side | Whichever number is larger | The height of the square
exercise: What is a map's key, or legend, for? | It explains the symbols, colours and lines | It tells you which way is north on the map | It gives the date the map was made | It lists the names of the people who live there
exercise: Which map would you use to see who owns the land? | A political map | A physical map | A contour map | A weather map
exercise: Which map would you use to see mountains and rivers? | A physical map | A political map | A road map of one town | A time zone map
exercise: What is the only kind of map that shows shapes, sizes, distances and directions all correctly? | A globe | A world map on paper | A phone map zoomed out | A big wall map
exercise: About how far is it around the Earth at the equator? | About 40,000 km | About 4,000 km | About 400,000 km | About 12,700 km
exercise: Who estimated the size of the Earth around 240 BC? | Eratosthenes, a Greek scholar | Christopher Columbus | Isaac Newton | Ptolemy's Roman soldiers, by pacing it out
exercise: What were portolan charts? | Sea maps with detailed coasts and compass lines | Star maps for finding the Pole Star at sea | Maps of castles drawn for kings | Road maps drawn for the marching Roman armies
exercise: How many satellites must a satnav hear from to fix its position? | At least four | Exactly one | At least twenty | None, it uses the mobile mast
exercise: How does EU5 show where the land is high? | With terrain pictures, a rough version of contour lines | With exact contour lines every 10 metres | It shows no height at all | With numbers on every hill and mountain top in metres
test: my cousin says people in 1337 thought the earth was flat lol. right? | No, educated people then knew it was round | Yes, everyone thought it was flat until 1492 | Yes, only sailors knew it was round | No, they thought it was a cube
test: Two hikers say "meet at grid square 4725". Why must both read eastings first? | Otherwise they could end up in different squares | Because eastings are always the smaller numbers | Because north is more important than east | It does not matter which comes first
test: In EU5 an army marches through six locations. What does that tell you about the distance in kilometres? | Nothing exact, because the game has no scale | Exactly 600 kilometres | Exactly 60 kilometres | It is always one day's walk
test: Why did portolan charts show coasts in great detail but leave inland areas almost empty? | Sailors needed the coasts, not the land far inside | Nobody lived inland in those days | Ink was too costly to waste on drawing the land | The inland areas were under the sea
test: Switching from EU5's political map mode to the trade map mode is most like what on a real map? | Using a different key over the same land | Changing the scale to 1:25,000 | Turning the map upside down | Moving the prime meridian
test: Mate says a 1:10,000,000 map is "large scale cos it covers loads". Is he right? | No, covering a huge area makes it small-scale | Yes, a bigger area always means a larger scale | Yes, scale means the size of the paper | No, it has no scale at all
test: If globes are the most accurate, why do people still mostly use flat maps? | Flat maps fold, fit in pockets and show detail | Globes are always wrong about sizes | Flat maps show no distortion at all | Globes were banned in schools
test: What would happen to a satnav if it could only hear one satellite? | It could not work out where it is | It would be twice as accurate | It would show the time but also the place exactly | Nothing, one satellite is enough
test: Why would an army be among the first to want very accurate maps of a country? | To plan marches and defend the land | To decorate the generals' tents | Because soldiers liked drawing | To count how many trees there were for firewood
test: Your map is 1:50,000 and the campsite is 6 cm away on the paper. How far is that? | 3 kilometres | 300 metres | 30 kilometres | 6 kilometres
test: A map has blue lines and blue patches. Without the key, what is your best guess? | Rivers and lakes | Motorways and car parks | Borders and capitals | Railways and stations
exam: Ascertain the distance upon the ground represented by a measured interval of four centimetres on a chart drawn to a scale of one to twenty-five thousand. | One kilometre | One hundred metres | Four kilometres | Twenty-five kilometres
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of closely spaced contour lines? | The terrain so depicted is steep | The terrain so depicted is level | The terrain so depicted lies below sea level | The terrain so depicted is wooded
exam: Identify the sole representation of the Earth that preserves shape, area, distance and direction simultaneously. | A globe | A political map | A physical map | A portolan chart
exam: It is given that a four-figure grid reference is to be stated. Determine which coordinate is to be given precedence. | The easting | The northing | The greater of the two | The altitude
